 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_01_ideal_gas_law`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Redlich and Kwong divided van der Waals&#x27;s attraction term by \sqrt{T}. This example draws CO2 P-V isotherms well above and just below the critical temperature from RedlichKwong, with VanDerWaals and IdealGas for comparison. Both real-gas equations are built from CO2&#x27;s critical temperature and pressure alone via from_critical_constants. Redlich-Kwong&#x27;s universal critical compressibility factor is 1/3, closer to real gases than van der Waals&#x27;s 3/8.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_redlich_kwong_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_03_redlich_kwong`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Redlich-Kwong isotherms of CO2 against van der Waals and the ideal gas</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_03_redlich_kwong`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Peng and Robinson tuned the temperature dependence of their attraction term, \alpha(T), to Pitzer&#x27;s acentric factor \omega so that the equation reproduces pure-fluid vapor pressures. This example computes the CO2 vapor-pressure curve predicted by PengRobinson (\omega = 0.224) with saturation_pressure, and compares it with van der Waals and Redlich-Kwong built from the same critical constants. Only Peng-Robinson passes through Pitzer&#x27;s defining point, \log_{10}(P_{sat}/P_c) = -1 - \omega at T/T_c = 0.7.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_04_peng_robinson_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_04_peng_robinson`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Peng-Robinson: vapor pressures from the acentric factor</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/plot_04_peng_robinson`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="G. N. Lewis replaced pressure by the fugacity f = \phi P so that the ideal-gas form \mu = \mu^\circ + RT\ln(f/P^\circ) stays exact for real fluids. The left panel shows the fugacity coefficient \phi of supercritical CO2 computed by fugacity_coefficient from three equations of state. The right panel shows Lewis&#x27;s phase-equilibrium criterion: below T_c the liquid and vapor fugacities from fugacity cross exactly at the saturation pressure.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/equations_of_state/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_05_lewis_fugacity_thumb.png
